gaps.description:
give_up() clears g2d_ok after 2,000,000 status reads, and VcrDdRoom then fails at once. The DP2 walk's 'drawable' requires g2d_ok, so every draw is skipped while the call still returns DD_OK (verified vcrdd_d3d.c:1859-1860; Clear2 at :1887 does the same). ContextCreate never checks g2d_ok, and flips continue. The game therefore keeps running behind a frozen picture.

This matches Max Payne on 10-04: frozen, pixel-identical frames with Num Lock alive. It is history-03's hypothesis H4, whose ring signature would be event 512 a=9.

After a give-up, DrvGetDirectDrawInfo withholds the HAL (vcrdd_d3d.c:2058), so every new CreateDevice fails with 8876086a (onbox-02, today). Recovery happens only at the next DrvEnableSurface or mode set (vcrdd.c:540).

Fix (Diag\D3DSafe bit 2):
- return a device-lost code from DP2, Clear2 and Flip;
- fail ContextCreate;
- log once;
- try the miniport's VcrHwResetEngine before withdrawing acceleration for the PDEV's life.
Hardware 'partial': an engine reset recovered today's stuck pipeline, but whether every wedge is recoverable is unknown.

VcrDd3dTarget and VcrDd3dState write only the legacy register set (verified vcrdd_3d.c:45-129). They never write:
- combineMode on FBI, TMU0 or TMU1, nor the 12-NOP exit from 2 pixels per clock;
- clipLeftRight1/BottomTop1;
- chromaKey/chromaRange, stipple, aaCtrl or chipMask.
stencilMode/stencilOp = 0 is written only for 32 bpp targets. Glide notes that combineMode bits act even with USE_COMBINE_MODE clear, and 3dfx's V5 HAL writes all three combineModes first at every state flush. Glide's extended combine and stencil outlive even a clean close.

The existing mitigations do not cover this:
- Diag\Reset3D (clears chipMask, sliCtrl, combineMode, aaCtrl and stencil on chip 0) is default off, with no 604 a=3 event in any committed evidence. It runs only at the exclusive owner's own release, never at DrvAssertMode, and refuses while the FIFO is on (history-26).
- The README's SliOffAaCtrl switch is also default off and was never run on silicon.
- The premise that a 16 bpp target ignores a stale STENCIL_ENABLE is unproven (history-27, hw-39). The spec's 'stencil only at 32 bpp' supports it without proving it.

Visual evidence of this class (visual-05, correlation only):
- Red Faction drew nothing right after GLQuake (09-29).
- Shogo showed its menu text on black without its backdrop after RTCW/Serious Sam (09-29, 10-01).
- H&D showed only its frame, then black, after Hexen II GL (10-01).
All three render fully in the Glide-free 11-08 sweeps. Shogo's text still drew, which the stale-FIFO mechanism (G01: no triangles at all) does not explain but stale combine or stencil state could (inferred). Today's register dump had these registers at 0, so they were not today's cause (hal-02).

Fix (Diag\D3DSafe bit 1): write a D3D-owned baseline once per DP2, pinned write-for-write in test_vcr_kmd_d3dseq.c:
- combineMode = 0, with the NOPs;
- clip1 = 0;
- chroma and stipple off;
- aaCtrl = 0;
- stencilMode/stencilOp = 0 at every depth.

Then verify:
- the A/B from visual-05: cold boot, run the title, run GLQuake and quit it cleanly, run the title again; repeat with Reset3D=1;
- the planned check of a 32 bpp GL session using stencil followed by d3dprobe at 16 bpp (hw-41 check 2).

What the code does (verified): prim_caps advertises TRANSPARENCY (vcrdd_d3d.c:2035), but nothing in the 3D path reads COLORKEYENABLE, DDRAWISURF_HASCKEYSRCBLT, V3D_CHROMAKEY or FZ_CHROMAKEY (grep: no hits). Colour keys exist only for DirectDraw blits (vcrdd_ddraw.c:434-456, 539-542). Per the DDK, the key is the AND of the render state and the texture's source key, and it is 'up to the driver'.

Visual evidence (visual-07, medium confidence): in the DX6 Thief II frame, trees and bushes are solid (0,0,8) silhouettes, where NewDark D3D9 shows them textured green. That is the engine Thief Gold 1.37 and System Shock 2 still run.

Hardware (hw-24):
- the FBI chroma compare runs after the texture lookup and bilinear filter, before the combine;
- TMU chroma substitution cannot coexist with TFACTOR or the extended combine (combineMode[30]);
- 3dfx therefore keyed in the FBI only and kept the TMU chroma registers for TFACTOR (D6DP2.C:6943).

The cap's history at 3dfx (history-17):
- removed for Napalm in 09/99 ('Napalm doesn't support texture chroma key');
- re-enabled in 12/99 as 'a required cap for whql', with the FBI implementation;
- colorKeyEnable defaulted TRUE (DX5 behaviour, inferred).
Bilinear fringes remain (inferred).

Fix (Diag\D3DFeat): load the FBI chromaKey from the texture's source key, expanded to xRGB888 by texture format; or stop advertising TRANSPARENCY. Test with a d3d7probe colorkey case, since D3D8 has no colour key.

The chain of events: caps_124 reports CheckDepthStencilMatch(X8R8G8B8, D16) = match. vcr_rt_format(rt32, 32, 16) = REFUSED (vcr_rtfmt.h:84-85), so target_of empties the target and ContextCreate returns DDERR_INVALIDPIXELFORMAT (vcrdd_d3d.c:1190-1196). D3D8 then reports 8876086c, and the ring logs 513/12 'ContextCreate refused: 32 bpp target with a 16-bit Z'.

What this breaks:
- fullscreen D3D8/9 at 32 bpp with D16;
- windowed devices with D16 on .124's 32 bpp desktop;
- SetRenderTarget pairings (silently);
- DX6/7 attachment of a 16-bit Z to a 32 bpp back buffer.
No title run so far hits it. At risk: Red Faction once its 32 bpp setting applies ('Unable to initialize DirectX 8.0 -- Device creation failed!'), BF1942 (which offers a '32bit depth buffer' choice), and fresh installs.

The chip cannot mix depths ('Napalm cannot handle a different depth zbuffer and rendering surface!'). 3dfx worked around it:
- allocated a 16-bit Z requested on a 32 bpp primary as 32-bit (24+8);
- relabelled or reallocated it at attach and adjusted depth fills;
- removed its ContextCreate check (D3CONTXT revision 12);
- listed D16 with ZSTENCIL_WITH_ARBITRARY_COLOR_DEPTH in its DX8 build.

Fix (Diag\D3DSafe bit 3): in Dd_CreateSurface, give a 16-bit Z created while rt32 holds and the target or desktop is 32 bpp 32-bit storage, and mark it so surf_zbits reports 32. Convert depth fills by MSB replication ((d<<8)|(d>>8)), not 3dfx's high-word copy.
Constraints:
- Keep D16 listed at the 32 bpp desktop for the proven 16 bpp fullscreen lane. The caps must stay mode-independent (runtime-22); 4a9793b broke this once.
- A locked promoted D16 returns 24-bit values (the runtime's 121a:0009 table keeps D16 as D16_LOCKABLE; 3dfx accepted this).
- The reverse pair R5G6B5 + D24 is already correct: D3D8 refuses it itself, and DX6/7 titles draw it 16/16 (be58b7d; runtime-04). Keep those tests.
Test: d3dprobe render --full --bpp 32 --zfmt d16, fullscreen and windowed, must reach 40/40. Each refused attempt costs two mode switches (G11), so run it attended.

For a fullscreen device the D3D8 runtime sets the display mode before it creates the buffers and calls ContextCreate. On a refusal it restores the desktop mode at once.

Today's case:
- 640x480x32 was set at 83009.846 s;
- the CRT was back at 1280x1024x32 at 83010.002 s, 156 ms later;
- d3dprobe's paced exit hold could not cover it.

A game that retries other Z formats or modes after a failure would flap the 1998 CRT; the project rule is at least 5 s between switches.

Mitigations:
- remove the refusals games can reach (G10), while keeping the refusal events 513/12, 15 and 17 loud;
- run probes of refusal cases once, attended. The on-box agent deliberately did not repeat this one.

What the code does (verified):
- Two stages compute (tex0 OP1 tex1), then OP0 with diffuse ('identical for MODULATE', vcrdd_d3d.c:843-848). That is wrong for a stage-1 ADD, SELECT, BLEND* or MODULATE2X.
- Stage 1 runs only when stage 0 is textured and stage 1 has its own texture (compute_regs :925-928). A stage 1 that uses only CURRENT or TFACTOR is ignored.
- BLENDTEXTUREALPHA and BLENDDIFFUSEALPHA are right only with TEXTURE as arg1.
- Every other colour op, and every alpha op except ADD and SELECT, becomes MODULATE (:714, :738-740, :862, :871-872).
UT2003 logs op 16 (BLENDCURRENTALPHA) on stage 1 once (history-12). TextureOpCaps advertises 7 ops (0x184f), against 3dfx's 20 (0x001effff).

Hardware: combineMode bit 31 (plus bit 30) in the FBI and both TMUs computes ((A'+B')*C'+D') << outshift, with invert modes x, -x, 1-x and x-1/2. That gives:
- ADD = SUB_CLOCAL + INVERT_LOCAL_X;
- ADDSIGNED via X_MINUS_HALF, and SUBTRACT via 0-X;
- ADDSMOOTH, BLEND{DIFFUSE,TEXTURE,FACTOR,CURRENT}ALPHA, BLENDTEXTUREALPHAPM and the MODULATE*_ADD* ops;
- TMU selects for iterated colour and alpha.
Limits:
- complement on arg2 is unavailable for ADDSIGNED, SUBTRACT and BLEND*ALPHA;
- D3D8 LERP and MULTIPLYADD fit only some argument sets (inferred);
- 3dfx offered 3 stages only on Voodoo3; a texture-less third stage in the FBI might be possible (inferred);
- PREMODULATE, BUMPENVMAP and DOT3 are impossible (G84).
Prerequisite: packed sARGB, so that TMU stages can see diffuse (G58).

Fix (hal Phase 3, Diag\D3DFeat):
- implement D3D's two-stage pipeline on combineMode, with stage 0 (including diffuse) before stage 1;
- handle stage 1 without a stage-0 texture;
- widen TextureOpCaps one op at a time as each probe passes.
Tests: d3dprobe stage1_add, stage1_select, blendta_rev, tint_stage1, addsigned and subtract, each against a computed reference.

Verified (vcrdd_d3d.c:1905-1910): every stage set validates, including the ones color_path and stage1_combine only approximate, and sets with more than 2 stages. Titles that pick a path by ValidateDevice therefore never take their fallbacks, and then degrade silently (MODULATE2X, op 16, the modifiers). Such titles: UE2, Halo (both inferred) and Max Payne, which logs 'D3D_OK stages:%d'.

3dfx refused, with D3DERR_TOOMANYOPERATIONS or an UNSUPPORTED* code:
- more than 2 stages;
- multitexture with trilinear, unless LOD_DITHER is set;
- D3DTA_SPECULAR as an argument;
- unsupported ops and complements.
The UT 3-stage remap (PRS 12099) shows 3dfx also met apps that never call it.

Fix (Diag\D3DFeat bit 0, hal Phase 2): answer exactly what compute_regs implements, and accept each op as G05/G12 implement it. Test: a d3dprobe 'validate' matrix, including ValidateDevice of MODULATE2X (runtime-27).

Today's timedemo on the fixed build 95678704 (onbox-07): at 60 s the glow around a blue crystal is a sharp-edged translucent teal rectangle; the texture's alpha shape is not applied.

On 09-28, on the build before the alignment fix (visual-12), the menu showed flat opaque squares where soft sprites belong:
- a 52x52 square of 4 dithered colours near (38,53,36), at x 593-646, y 588-641;
- two more hard-edged squares near (690,140) and (710,10).
visual-12 attributed those to the since-fixed alignment bug. Today's frame shows the class persists, so that attribution is at best partial.

Candidates:
- D3DTA_ALPHAREPLICATE dropped (G13);
- a stage alpha-source or blend-mapping error (G12, G28);
- a small DXT3/5 alpha texture;
- a missing luminance/alpha format (G34).
The rest of the cutscenes render largely right: ring and planet at 30 s, the escape pod with smoke over trees at 180 s, crisp subtitles.

Diagnose by capturing log_combo (513/21-23) during the scene.

Verified: depth is enabled only for ZENABLE == D3DZB_TRUE (vcrdd_d3d.c:986). An app that asks for a W-buffer without checking caps therefore draws with no depth test at all. WINFO is skipped by size (:1556), WBUFFER is not advertised, and d3dprobe has no USEW test (history-42).

Hardware:
- fbzMode[3] W-buffering: a 4.12 float at 16 bpp, 24-bit at 32 bpp;
- fbzMode[21] chooses W or Z as the source of that float;
- the W used is the FBI's Wb, which also indexes the fog table.

What 3dfx did:
- mapped USEW to WBUFFER + ENDEPTHBUFFER;
- remapped W from WINFO into [1, 65520];
- advertised WBUFFER and WFOG;
- moved vertex fog from W to the Z iterator under a W-buffer (D3FOG.C FnZTable cases 5 and 8). Our synthetic-1/W vertex-fog carrier (G32) must move the same way.

Which titles set USEW is unmeasured (inferred: Dark engine, DX6 titles, UE2, Halo).

Cheap interim fix: treat USEW as a Z-buffer until the W-buffer lands. Test: d3dprobe wbuffer.

What the code does (verified):
- the Z-format list includes D24S8 (vcrdd_d3d.c:2204-2212);
- dwStencilCaps is never set;
- compute_regs reads no stencil state;
- clear_rects ignores D3DCLEAR_STENCIL;
- 32 bpp targets write stencilMode = stencilOp = 0 (G03).
Apps that check StencilCaps see nothing and fall back. Apps that only check the format get a silent no-op stencil test, which is wrong for masking uses such as mirrors, portals and shadows (inferred).

Hardware: 8-bit stencil at 32 bpp only (renderMode[1:0]=2, a 24+8 aux word).
- stencilMode holds ref [7:0], mask [15:8], write mask [23:16], enable [24] and function [27:25].
- stencilOp holds fail/zfail/zpass, which match D3DSTENCILOP 1-8.
- With the enable clear the chip behaves as ALWAYS/REPLACE, so the disabled state must be written as 0 (3dfx does; so does our 32 bpp setup).
- FASTFILL clears all 8 stencil bits with no per-bit mask; a masked clear needs a 2D blit with a byte mask, as Glide does.
- Two-sided stencil is impossible: there is a single stencilOp register (inferred).
3dfx advertised all 8 ops and stripped STENCIL_ENABLE below 32 bpp.

Users: Halo (two-sided CCW_STENCIL* states) and BF1942 (SStencilEnable). UT2003/2004 are staged UseStencil=False. Count STENCILENABLE per context before investing (G76).

Fix (Diag\D3DFeat):
- map the stencil states to the registers at 32 bpp;
- do D3DCLEAR_STENCIL as a fastfill with REF/WMASK;
- report the stencil caps.
Test: d3dprobe stencil (32 bpp, D24S8).

Today: texP8 = 0 in the caps. The DP2 walker skips SETPALETTE by size and UPDATEPALETTE by its counts (vcrdd_d3d.c:1557, 1716-1724). UPDATEPALETTE is parsed wStateCount times, although the DDK sends exactly one record regardless (hal-27); low risk while P8 stays unlisted.

What the spec defines:
- textureMode format 5 (P8), 6 (P8 expanded to ARGB6666, the only alpha palette) and 14 (AP88 = A8P8);
- a 256-entry palette written through the nccTable0 I/Q registers with bit 31 set;
- four texels looked up at once, so bilinear filtering works on P8.
Glide writes 0x80000000 | ((i & 0xFE) << 23) | rgb into nccTable0[4..11] and broadcasts one palette to both TMUs.

Why it is still unproven:
- the vintage lane saw P8 sample white through 3dfx's D3D HAL on Napalm (GoldSrc in D3D: a white world under an RGB sky) and compiled P8/A8P8 out (DISABLE_PAL8_ON_NAPALM);
- 3dfx itself disabled and re-enabled palettized textures (revisions 24/25), and left its DX7 A8P8 entry permanently off;
- our OpenGL ICD runs Counter-Strike with FX_NO_PALETTED_TEXTURE=1;
- the cause is unknown (the palette write path, the chip field, or the driver); Glide's own Napalm palette path suggests a driver bug (inferred).

Titles that would take P8 if offered:
- Shogo ('Couldn't find suitable 8 bit texture format', MasterPaletteMode, 'Palette alpha');
- AvP ('chosen %u-bit palettized texture format');
- Rainbow Six (CheckForPalettized / loadtex);
- H&D (P8/A8P8 enumerated);
- UE1 D3DDrv (UsePalettes=True).
All fall back to 16-bit RGB: double the texture memory and lost palette tricks (inferred). Low priority with 32 MB per chip.

Prove first: a d3dprobe P8 quad, or Glide grTexDownloadTable with a GR_TEXFMT_P_8 texture, on .124.

ContextCreate stores pd = lpGbl->dhpdev, and DrvDisablePDEV frees the PDEV. Three paths use the old pointer later:
- ctx_free calls VcrDd2dSync(c->pd);
- DP2 and Clear2 dereference c->pd;
- a mip chain is freed with the heap pointer saved at allocation.
If dxg delivers a call on an old context, or frees a chain, after a dynamic mode change or an alt-tab, kernel memory is used after it was freed (inferred). Whether dxg tears contexts down first is unverified, and alt-tab was never tested.

Fix:
- scrub the g_ctx entries in DrvDisablePDEV/DrvDisableDirectDraw;
- re-derive pd on every call;
- add an 86Box alt-tab test.

Today: w3() and vertex() write setup registers straight to the PCI FIFO. The register window is mapped uncached; the LFB is write-combined. The FIFO holds 31 entries (measured), about 2.5 vertices, so the CPU spins through each triangle's rasterisation while the chip idles during software T&L. Frame time is roughly CPU + GPU rather than the larger of the two (inferred). Long display-lock holds fit 'everything crawled while Max Payne ran' (inferred link, G04).

What 3dfx's HAL and our h5 Glide do instead: stream a CMDFIFO in frame-buffer memory (the lower 16 MB only), with hole counting.
- state as PK1/PK4 packets with chip fields;
- textures as PK5;
- triangles as PK3 vertex runs (up to 16 vertices per header; independent, strip or fan; CMD_CONT continuation);
- the read pointer is read only when room runs out;
- there are two independent streams.
Packet 6 and the AGP paths are unusable on this PCI-66 board (hw-14).

Reusable host-tested logic: GBK/gbk/gbk_fifo.c (room, wrap, jump packet; the 10-store init with hole counting) and gbk_packet.c (masked headers). Do not copy from gbkernel.c:
- its readPtrL read on every reserve, which recreates G48;
- its no-op fence: our LFB is write-combined, so a real store fence is needed every 64K dwords and around the wrap jump;
- its 10M-iteration wait; use the 2M SPIN_CAP.

Hazards (perf-18):
- detect a foreign (Glide) FIFO and refuse to arm, or reset it (G01);
- drain and disarm before any direct 2D, GDI, mode-set or Glide access;
- never mix direct and FIFO writes;
- keep every wait bounded, with a fallback to the direct path;
- never make a request larger than one ring lap (the vendor's 3DMark2000 TDR hypothesis).

Expected (inferred, perf section 4.1): 1-2 M triangles/s for 1-texture lists and 3-6 M for strips, against about 120-220K today.

Fix: P6 (Diag D3DCmdFifo: a ring at 96 KB, packet 3, fenced write-combined writes); README roadmap item 6.

vertex() calls VcrDdRoom before each vertex: one uncached status read, plus one more per spin. It then writes sVx, sVy, four float colours, sVz, sOowfbi and 3 registers per texture stage, followed by sBeginTriCMD/sDrawTriCMD (verified, vcrdd_3d.c:183-239). That is 27 writes per untextured triangle, 36 single-textured and 45 dual-textured, with at least 3 reads, for every primitive type.

Cost model (inferred):
- a write costs 45-150 ns;
- a read costs about 1 us (a vendor comment says so), and also waits for every posted write ahead of it;
- so a textured triangle costs about 4.6-8.4 us, or 120-220K triangles/s with the CPU fully in the HAL;
- that is about 700-1,300 textured triangles per 85 Hz frame at 50% CPU.

Interim fix without a CMDFIFO (P3, Diag D3DFastSetup): count FIFO credit from one status read, as the text path's VCR_MONO.credit already does. The measured symptom is G46.

walk() turns TRIANGLESTRIP, TRIANGLEFAN, INDEXEDTRIANGLELIST(2), INDEXEDTRIANGLESTRIP/FAN and TRIANGLEFAN_IMM into tri()/imm_tri() calls (verified, vcrdd_d3d.c:1612-1689).

Unused hardware and techniques:
- the setup unit's own strip/fan continuation (sSetupMode SM_FAN / SM_NO_PINGPONG; one vertex plus sDrawTriCMD per added triangle);
- packet-3 strips (G47);
- 3dfx's autostrip (shared-edge detection, then 1-vertex continuation packets).
Using them saves up to 3x the vertex writes on strip and fan content.

Constraints:
- keep the odd-triangle winding equivalent; culling is untested because d3dprobe forces CULLNONE (G74);
- flat shading defeats strip reuse (G24).

Fix: P3 in the direct path, then packet 3 in P6.

Today all 3D writes go to chip 0 with snooping off. Reset3D writes sliCtrl = 0, IOCTL_VCR_SLI is reached only through Glide's SLI_AA_REQUEST, and the miniport already drives 4-chip SLI for Glide (vcrmp_sli.c).

What 3dfx did:
- creating a 3D flip chain calls Enter_3DApplication, which promotes the primary to the overlay (desktop flips cannot be pipelined, and Napalm SLI needs the overlay) and calls Promote_DeviceToSLIAA;
- Compute_SLIAA_Config defaults to maximum performance: SLI_AA_CONFIGURATION 5 (4-way SLI, AA off) on a quad board, also the 3dfx Tools default;
- trilinear multitexture got per-chip LOD bias offsets (0/0.25/0.5/0.75).

The numbers: Glide measured 330 Mpix/s on one chip against 1,124.6 on four. At 1280x960 with overdraw 2.5 (inferred), fill takes 18.4 ms at 1 px/clk, 9.2 ms with 2PPC and 2.7 ms on four chips. One chip cannot hold 85 fps on overdraw-heavy scenes even with 2PPC (estimated ceiling: about 1.33 Gpix/s on 4 chips at 2 px/clk vs 0.167 today).

Caveats:
- FASTFILL runs at single-chip speed in SLI (a hardware bug); use 2D blits for clears;
- dithering breaks at band height 1;
- y-origin flips need care;
- render-target textures need SLI off (inferred);
- textures are replicated per chip;
- SLI does not help frames limited by the CPU or the setup unit (G46).
The user chose no AA, so the target is the non-AA 4-chip shape.

This is the highest-risk step, given the SLI/AA wedge history: do it last and supervised (P9). Interim option: stage the heavy titles at 1024x768.

Halo 1.10 is D3D9 per the games report; the HAL report lists it as D3D8.

The menu: it reached its textured main menu at 1280x960 only after D3D32 + D3DBigTex=7 and the DXT2/DXT4 aliasing (4ef226e; before that the menu drew white).

The three workarounds:
- config.txt DisableRenderTargets for 0x121a:0x0009, without which it stops at FATAL 105 (G36);
- -novideo whenever vcrmp runs, because Bink runs about 10x slow (G56);
- requires.json declares an SSE2 floor, so the gate refuses .124 and an operator override deploys it. FINDINGS says the floor is 'at least partly wrong', but not to relax it before gameplay is seen.
The menu ignores synthetic input, so gameplay needs a person.

Today's timedemo rendered in-engine cutscenes largely right, but did not finish in 485 s, and showed glow squares (G17) and rock noise (G06).

Gameplay will exercise:
- per-card decal depth bias (DecalZBiasValue / DecalSlopeZBiasValue; G22);
- two-sided stencil (CCW_STENCIL*; G35);
- cube-map reflections (G84);
- specular (G25), environment fog, and MinMaxBlendOpIsBroken (G91).
Its -useff/-use00 shader paths and config flags can pin a safe path for 0x121a:0x0009.

A 60 s sweep frame on 13256e46 was exactly one colour, (255,255,255) (visual-10). The plan calls it the animated menu, but the menu's white phase keeps its logo, text and cursor, so recapture with fbshot --probe.

.124 is 'blk' for Halo in the LAN rounds.

Every armed sweep frame of Rainbow Six was black with the RAINBOW SIX ERROR dialog: 09-28 07:05, 09-29, 10-01, 11-08 00:48 and 11-08 02:08. The ring logged 'ContextCreate refused: 16 bpp target with a 32-bit Z'.

The 16-over-32 fix (be58b7d, vcrdd 95678704) landed at 02:41, after the 02:08 'fixed driver' re-sweep, and applies only with D3D32 armed. Its only proof is a 960x240 strip of three 320x240 thumbnails: intro, then menu. The compat DB still says 'untested'.

Needed: full-resolution menu and in-game frames on 95678704, and a recorded compat row.

In game it may hit:
- colour-keyed surfaces (G09);
- palettized textures (G38);
- planning-overlay lines (G16);
- fog.
Its software renderer (DevicePicker) is the fallback.

SafeDisc 2.80.010 in Mods\bf1942\Mod.dll raises 'Cannot locate the CD-ROM', and DAEMON Tools 3.47 cannot satisfy that version. The client therefore never reaches the HAL in single player. The LAN host launcher runs the dedicated server; a join needs a partner. The sweep frames show only the SafeDisc dialog, the server console and an empty desktop.

If the protection is ever solved, the PCDX8 renderer:
- warns that it 'only officially supports cards that supports HTL' (likely a modal at every launch, inferred);
- uses cube maps (G84), stencil (G35) and DXT1-5;
- runs vertex shaders in software;
- offers a '32bit depth buffer' choice (G10).

Not covered by any d3dprobe check:
- alpha test, and every blend factor except SRCALPHA/INVSRCALPHA and SRCALPHA/ZERO;
- Z functions other than LESSEQUAL, ZWRITE off, and USEW;
- culling (the probe forces CULLNONE, so the strip odd-winding swap is untested);
- TRIANGLELIST/FAN, the indexed forms and TRIANGLEFAN_IMM (the probe draws only DrawPrimitiveUP);
- CLAMP/WRAP beyond [0,1], bilinear correctness, and the 64 mip chain on silicon;
- TFACTOR, the BLEND*ALPHA ops, stage-0 ADD and the alpha ops;
- EXP/EXP2 fog on silicon, dither, sub-rect clears;
- render-to-texture, a lost device, ValidateDevice, lines and points;
- multi-level DXT/8888 chains, and DXT in stage 1.

Probe limitations:
- it reads only the top-left 256x256 of the back buffer;
- its textures land where its own allocation pattern puts them, which is why it missed the alignment bug;
- its *_def tests always fail under D3D8 (LockRect on D3DPOOL_DEFAULT returns 8876086c; a probe expectation, not a HAL defect);
- its perf scene (200 triangles a frame) only measures vsync;
- d3dprobe_run.py has no --pace option (d3dprobe_paced.py wraps it).

Caps mode does not report: StencilCaps, LineCaps, Caps2, PresentationIntervals, MaxStreams, MaxPointSize, the guard band, MaxTextureRepeat; the formats X8R8G8B8, X1R5G5B5, X4R4G4B4, L8, A8, A8L8, A4L4, R3G3B2, A8P8 and D16_LOCKABLE; D3DUSAGE_RENDERTARGET on R5G6B5, A8R8G8B8 and X8R8G8B8 textures (expected yes/yes/no); D3DUSAGE_DYNAMIC; or ValidateDevice of MODULATE2X.

