G37.risk:
Default 16 bpp path, unarmed: none. Same register traffic, allocation and flush points; only the context-gone log text gains two counters.

Armed:
- extra flushes, using the sequence proven since 09-26, only for textures the engine wrote or that were rebound after being written while unbound;
- one status-read VcrDd2dSync per such texture before a CPU write-back, the same idle wait every Lock and TEXBLT already does;
- no caps or allocation change.

Glide/OpenGL coexistence: none. Marking touches system memory only, and prepare returns before any flush while a Glide process owns the chip (w->drawable false, vcrdd_d3d.c:1325-1326, 1859-1860). Like every D3D path, it still relies on G01's FIFO state.

SLI: none today, because D3D runs on chip 0. G51 must carry three things:
- the flush must reach every chip's TMUs;
- a render-target texture needs SLI off (hw Q12);
- a 2D blit into a texture must reach every chip's copy.

Flip path: none. Flip-chain surfaces are not textures; the marking function returns at the caps test.

Performance: at most one drain plus 7 writes per engine-written texture per bind-after-write, and one per pass for ping-pong post-processing. That is negligible against G46's 123 ms frames (inferred; boot B's perf and game fps confirm).

Failure under a wedged chip: VcrDd2dSync can give up and turn acceleration off, exactly as a Lock can. That is G02's silent give-up, not a new mode.

The hazard the design avoids: without the sync, marking engine writes would let VcrDd3dTexFlush's CPU read-back write a stale dword over fresh texels (row 0). That is why the sync is in the plan and why the probes check all of row 0.

Future work that must keep this:
- G53's register shadowing must invalidate its texBaseAddr shadow after a flush and must never shadow the flush away;
- G47's CMDFIFO must keep the flush and the sync-before-write-back rule;
- G56 should reuse SF_ENGINE (3dfx's BltToTxtrInFifo).

G43: the new calls use c->pd as the rest of the walk does, so there is no new use-after-free exposure.

