WHAT IT DRAWS. A grid of 64x64 cells in the 256x256 window. Each cell is one quad (2 triangles) with exactly one state, reset to the baseline after its cell:
- c0: baseline MODULATE(TEXTURE, DIFFUSE) on a 565 64x64 texture, pixel-checked;
- c1, c2: stage-0 MODULATE2X / MODULATE4X (G05);
- c3: stage-1 MODULATE2X with two textures (G05);
- c4, c5: TEXTURE|ALPHAREPLICATE and TEXTURE|COMPLEMENT (G13);
- c6: MODULATE(TEXTURE, TFACTOR) (G14);
- c7: SUBTRACT (G12);
- c8: stage-1 BLENDCURRENTALPHA (G12);
- c9: SRCBLEND SRCCOLOR (G29);
- c10: DESTBLEND DESTALPHA (G28);
- c11: SHADEMODE FLAT (G24);
- c12: FILLMODE WIREFRAME (G24);
- c13: ZBIAS 4 (G22);
- c14: ZENABLE USEW (G23);
- c15: SPECULARENABLE with a specular FVF (G25);
- c16: ADDRESSU MIRROR (G33);
- c17: MIPFILTER LINEAR on a 64 chain (G26);
- c18: MIPMAPLODBIAS and MAXMIPLEVEL (G27);
- c19: STENCILENABLE (G35);
- c20: FOGTABLEMODE LINEAR, start 0.1, end 0.9 in Z units (G31);
- c21: TEXTURETRANSFORMFLAGS COUNT2|PROJECTED (G92);
- c22: DITHERENABLE, 32 bpp runs only (G30);
- last: 4 LINELIST lines, 4 POINTLIST points (G16) and one ValidateDevice (G15).

HOW IT IS CHECKED. The runner records pre_seq before the run, fetches 'vcrctl log <pre_seq>' into a file after it, downloads that file, and runs 'd3dcensus.py --expect tools/d3dprobe_census_expect.json'.

PASS:
- the census equals the expected table EXACTLY. With G76 deployed: every key present, 2 triangles per cell, 4 lines, 4 points, 1 validate, and no other gap key;
- before G76: the log_combo keys of c1-c8, one set-up each (c3 and c8 by their stage-1 op only); c10's DSTALPHA as programmed; c9's SRCCOLOR and every render-state key reading UNMEASURED, never 0; no other gap key;
- context-gone triangles equal the count the probe prints;
- 0 unparsed;
- complete: true;
- c0's pixels within ±8 per channel of the computed MODULATE reference, the 'modulate' rule at 565.

The other cells are not pixel-checked; each gap's own test owns their pixels. This calibrates the instrument against known input before it is trusted on games.

