- DXT5 fonts 512xN;
- A8R8G8B8 textures up to 1024x512 (UT2003 combos log).

With the Voodoo3-era HAL (256x256, no FOURCC), UT2004's device died at CreateTexture (D3DERR_INVALIDCALL). It also ended in 'Critical Error' in the 09-28 D3D32-only sweep.

.124 has D3DBigTex=7 and D3D32=1 armed. A fresh install does not, so these titles cannot run there until the defaults change.

Halo 1.10 (D3D9, loaded dynamically) reached its textured main menu only after three things:
- D3D32=1 and D3DBigTex=7 armed;
- DXT2/DXT4 offered: the menu was white until the HAL listed them;
- the staged config.txt flag DisableRenderTargets for Vendor 0x121a / 0x0009 'Voodoo 4 and 5'. Without it, Halo stops at FATAL dialog 105 (render-target creation failing).

So Halo has hard requirements on default-off switches and runs without render-target effects. Gameplay has never been seen on .124.

What Halo uses in gameplay:
- per-card decal depth bias: DecalZBiasValue / DecalSlopeZBiasValue, DEPTHBIAS;
- two-sided stencil: CCW_STENCIL* states;
- cube-map environment reflections;
- specular (DisableSpecular flag);
- environment fog;
- blend ops (MinMaxBlendOpIsBroken).

We advertise none of these, so gameplay depends on Halo's fallbacks. Its -useff/-use00 shader paths and config flags can pin a safe path for 0x121a:0x0009.

requires.json declares SSE2 and the gate refuses .124; an operator override deploys it. FINDINGS: do not relax the floor before gameplay is seen.

The D3D8 driver module's strings show what it checks:
- texop caps ADD, BLENDTEXTUREALPHA, BUMPENVMAP, DISABLE, MODULATE, SELECTARG1/2 (we lack only the bump ops);
- DXT1-5, cube maps, DRV_setRenderTarget;
- software vertex shaders, 'D3D_OK stages:%d'.

The staged registry forces 32 bpp, so D3D32 is a hard requirement. Only the main menu has been seen on our HAL; the tutorial level was played on the GeForce2 in 2026-08.

The H&D launcher writes 'Display bitdepth' 32 into HKLM. Before D3D32 existed it failed with 'Unable to initialize graphics'. With D3D32 it reaches its profile screen at 1280x960 (11-08).

Its LS3D renderer (i3d2.dll) uses:
- uncompressed lightmaps;
- bump maps (falls back without a bump format);
- reflection maps and fog;
- P8/A8P8/DXT/D24S8 formats, enumerated.

Stage-1 ops beyond SELECT/ADD/MODULATE map to a TMU multiply (inferred risk for its layering). In-game was only seen on the GeForce2.

Rainbow Six (DX5/6 IM through DirectDraw, 640x480x16) takes the deepest Z from the 32 bpp desktop's list. The 11-08 vcr_rt_format change draws that pair as 16/16 and it now reaches intro and menu.

The binary also uses:
- colour keys: Surface::SetTransparentValue, 'colorkey';
- palettized textures: CheckForPalettized, 'loadtex' palette;
- fog (readme).

If its textures are colour-keyed, our HAL ignores COLORKEYENABLE (see games-26). Its software renderer (DevicePicker) is the fallback.

Shogo runs at 1280x960x16 (screendepth 16). d3d.ren:
- looks for an 8-bit palettized texture format ('Couldn't find suitable 8 bit texture format', MasterPaletteMode). We offer no P8, so it uses 16-bit;
- checks 'Src blend SRCCOLOR' and 'Table fog' / 'Palette alpha';
- picks 1-pass (2 TMU) or 2-pass lightmapping;
- enables env maps.

The VSA-100 can do P8 and the same-colour source blend (GR_BLEND_SAME_COLOR_EXT; 3dfx advertised SRCCOLOR as a source factor on Napalm). Menu PASS on 11-08; in game never seen. compat (GeForce era) noted the client menu renders intermittently.

The launcher checks the Diag switches in the registry. Without D3D32 and D3DBigTex 0x5/7/d/f it moves cam_ext.cfg aside, and the DX6 path only reaches 640x480. With them, NewDark's D3D9 display rendered a mission at 1280x960x32 on 09-28 (before the 11-08 fixes).

The staged cam_ext.cfg asks for:
- multisampletype 8, against the user's no-AA choice;
- tex_filter_mode 16 and tex_filter_trilinear;
- d3d_disp_enable_distortionfx (render targets);
- d3d_disp_enable_atoc.

We offer none of these, so it relies on NewDark's fallbacks (inferred). Particles are already staged as disks rather than points. vsync_mode is at its default (on).

The client never reaches Direct3D: SafeDisc 2.80 in Mods\bf1942\Mod.dll raises 'Cannot locate the CD-ROM'. The dedicated server runs.

Once past that, the PCDX8 renderer:
- warns that it 'only officially supports cards that supports HTL' (likely a modal per launch, inferred);
- uses cube maps, which this hardware cannot do;
- uses stencil states and DXT1-5;
- runs vertex shaders in software;
- offers a '32bit depth buffer' choice, relevant to the D16@32 issue.

ValidateDevice is told every stage set works, including sets the HAL then draws differently. In the stage mapping:
- MODULATE2X/4X are drawn as MODULATE;
- stage-1 BLEND*ALPHA and SUBTRACT become a TMU multiply;
- D3DTA_COMPLEMENT/ALPHAREPLICATE are masked off by ARG_KIND;
- a stage 1 without a texture is dropped.

UT2003 uses MODULATE2X although it is not in TextureOpCaps, and Max Payne logs 'D3D_OK stages:%d'. Engines that choose a path by validation (UE2, Halo, Max Payne; inferred for UE2/Halo) are steered into the wrong one. 3dfx implements the modifiers via combineMode INVERT/AREP.

texP8 is 0 in the live caps. Several engines would take P8 if it were offered:
- Shogo ('8 bit texture format');
- AvP (palettized format choice);
- Rainbow Six (CheckForPalettized/loadtex);
- H&D (P8/A8P8 enumerated);
- UE1 D3DDrv (UsePalettes=True).

All fall back to 16-bit RGB, which doubles texture memory and loses palette tricks (inferred).

The VSA-100 has P8 and AP88 with a downloadable palette, and 3dfx advertised ALPHAPALETTE. Low priority with a 64 MB chip.

The gaps:
- lines and points are parsed and skipped (vcrdd_d3d.c:1690-1715);
- MODULATE2X/4X fall to MODULATE (:714);
- the alpha blend is always ONE/ZERO (:998);
- ALPHAREPLICATE is masked off.
No frame contains an in-game HUD line, particle, lightmapped scene or translucency on a current build, so none of these can be seen or ruled out.
Two partial checks: Thief II DX6 is no darker than D3D9 (mean luma 6.9 vs 8.4), and no missing line or point is visible anywhere.

CheckDepthStencilMatch(X8R8G8B8, D16) returns yes. The 16-bit Z is created without objection: CanCreateSurface refuses non-16-bit Z only when rt32 is off, and CreateSurface leaves the Z to DirectDraw. Then target_of -> vcr_rt_format(rt32, 32, 16) = REFUSED -> ContextCreate returns DDERR_INVALIDPIXELFORMAT, and D3D8 reports D3DERR_INVALIDCALL.

This breaks:
- fullscreen D3D8/9 at 32 bpp with D16;
- windowed devices on .124's 32 bpp desktop with D16;
- SetRenderTarget pairings (silently: nothing draws);
- DX6/7 attachments of a 16-bit Z to a 32 bpp back buffer.

3dfx allocated a 16-bit Z at 32 bpp with 32-bit storage (at create, and again at attach) and removed its ContextCreate mismatch error.

Fix, behind a default-off switch: in Dd_CreateSurface, give a 16-bit Z created while rt32 and pd->bpp==32 hold 32-bit storage and mark it; make surf_zbits return 32 for marked surfaces; keep D16 listed. Test with d3dprobe --bpp 32 --zfmt d16, fullscreen and windowed.

For legacy drivers the runtime builds format ops itself (d3d8 0x6d9af134-0x6d9af464). Render-target ops come only from a 171-entry table keyed by PCI vendor/device (d3d8 VA 0x6d9a1700). The registry override only switches flags off: HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Direct3D\ForceDriverFlagsOff, and the 'on' mask is forced to 0.

The entry for 121a:0009 is 0x4580:
- R5G6B5 and A8R8G8B8 render-target textures, with OFFSCREEN_RENDERTARGET (A8R8G8B8 only while D3DBigTex bit 2 lists it);
- D16 kept as D16_LOCKABLE;
- no ZSTENCIL_WITH_ARBITRARY_COLOR_DEPTH.

X8R8G8B8 render-target textures are never granted. Halo needs DisableRenderTargets (FATAL 105). Its failure is likely an X8R8G8B8 render target or a non-power-of-2 size, but that is unmeasured. Only a DX8 DDI (GETFORMAT) could add X8R8G8B8 render targets.

The query arrives at every enable (42e02f16, 0x8c bytes = DD_GETHEAPALIGNMENTDATA). Because we decline it:
- textures are placed 4-byte aligned; this broke UT2003's fonts, worked around by mipchain_ext, but only on the D3DBigTex path;
- default-path single-level textures and render-target textures still keep DirectDraw's placement;
- 3D targets and Z buffers get no pitch alignment, so a windowed client with an odd width is refused.

Fix: answer Texture/ZBuffer/FlipTarget/Offscreen with 16-byte start and pitch alignment. That XP honours the answer is inferred; the placement log verifies it.

dwDeviceRenderBitDepth is DDBD_16 unless rt32. Without it:
- CheckDeviceType(HAL, X8R8G8B8) fails;
- windowed D3D8/9 at .124's 32 bpp desktop cannot create a device;
- 32-bit-only titles fail (H&D Deluxe: 'Unable to initialize graphics');
- NewDark's D3D9 display needs D3D32 plus BigTex bits 0 and 2.

The path was 40/40 today once the stale FIFO was cleared, so D3D32 should become the default after P0 and runtime-02.

Gains:
- A8 textures;
- X8R8G8B8 render-target textures and our own Z ops;
- Caps2 (DYNAMICTEXTURES, FULLSCREENGAMMA, CANRENDERWINDOWED), PresentationIntervals and MaxPointSize;
- per-process DXVERSION.

Costs:
- the runtime stops emulating state blocks above DDI type 5 (or set EmulateStateBlocks=1);
- the DX8 DP2 tokens and vertex/index buffer handles if MaxStreams >= 1 (whether MaxStreams=0 keeps DX7 tokens is unverified);
- new D3D9 declaration-conversion refusals;
- 3dfx's NT DX8 path never ran;
- every D3D8/9 title moves at once.

Revisit only if Halo turns out to need X8R8G8B8 render targets or A8.

understand:vendor.summary:
3dfx's own W2K/XP Direct3D HAL for the VSA-100 is a DX7 DDI only (DX=7; GetDriverInfo2 and D3DCAPS8 exist only in a DX8 build 3dfx never shipped on NT). D3D8 titles therefore see runtime-built caps on both 3dfx's driver and ours, so 3dfx's DX7 caps are the right yardstick.
1. Today's X8R8G8B8 + D16 CreateDevice failure on .124 has a vendor answer. 3dfx never refuses a depth/target size mismatch: it allocates or relabels the Z to the target's depth and adjusts depth fills. Our ContextCreate refuses the pair. Convert fills by MSB replication, not 3dfx's high-word copy.
2. All 20 texture ops 3dfx advertises (we advertise 7) come from one register: combineMode with bit 31 set in the FBI and both TMUs. Its fields give output shift (2X/4X), invert stages (ADD, ADDSIGNED, SUBTRACT, COMPLEMENT), select+1 (ALPHAREPLICATE) and TMU iterated/constant inputs. TFACTOR is also loaded into the TMUs' chromaRange. Our HAL writes combineMode = 0.
3. Silicon features 3dfx uses and we do not:
   - stencil (32 bpp only);
   - W-buffer: ours turns depth OFF on ZENABLE=USEW, a correctness bug;
   - Z bias through zaColor;
   - MIRROR addressing;
   - SAMECOLOR blend factors: our "the chip cannot" comment for SRCCOLOR/DESTCOLOR is wrong on VSA-100;
   - single-pass trilinear using both TMUs on one chain;
   - LOD bias and MAXMIPLEVEL, guard band, and 2ppc at 16 bpp with its 12-NOP exit rule.
4. Done in several passes or as substitutes: textured specular is a second additive pass; lines and points are 1-pixel quads (ours drops them); the texture colour key is the FBI chromaKey, tested after filtering and on by default. Legacy TEXTUREMAPBLEND is mapped to stage 0 by 3dfx and ignored by ours.
5. Do not copy:
   - the NT TEXTURETRANSFORMFLAGS bug that overwrites ADDRESSU/V (Win9x fixed it);
   - destination-alpha factors replaced by ONE/ZERO on every draw;
   - Z_ACCESS_OPT;
   - the missing ZTEST and MIPMAPLODBIAS caps;
   - stage 0's MAXMIPLEVEL used for both TMUs;
   - lines drawn from y-1 to y.
6. Performance: 3dfx runs fullscreen D3D in 4-way SLI by default on a 4-chip board, via overlay promotion. It streams a frame-buffer CMDFIFO with packet-3 vertices and caps queued swaps at 6 to avoid a hang. Our D3D is one chip writing PCI registers one by one.
7. Never in 3dfx's driver: hardware T&L (its T&L HAL is CPU code, not built on NT), anisotropy, cube maps, bump/DOT3, border addressing, point sprites.
8. Evidence gaps: the fogMode FOG_Z|FOG_ALPHA conflict between the spec and the shipped driver, the cause of P8 sampling white, and the reason for the dest-alpha substitution (PRS 8373).
The report gives path:line for every claim and a prioritised, Diag-gated plan in section 15.

items.detail:
3dfx sets USE_COMBINE_MODE (bit 31) and DISABLE_CHROMA_SUBSTITUTION (bit 30) in the FBI and both TMUs for every draw. The extended combine fields provide:
- OUTSHIFT 2x/4x: MODULATE2X/4X and ADDSIGNED2X;
- INVERT_LOCAL/INVERT_OTHER: ADD = SUB_CLOCAL + INVERT_LOCAL_X; ADDSIGNED uses X_MINUS_HALF; SUBTRACT uses the default 0-X; also ADDSMOOTH and complements;
- TMU selects for iterated colour/alpha and chroma-range constants: BLENDFACTORALPHA, BLENDCURRENTALPHA, and diffuse at stage 0.
3dfx advertises 20 ops (0x001effff) against our 7 (0x184f). PREMODULATE, BUMPENVMAP(L) and DOTPRODUCT3 are unsupported. The per-op register recipes are in the stage-0 table.

Every texture is allocated once, as HOME_BOTH. With MIPFILTER LINEAR on one stage:
- TMU1 samples the odd LODs (tLOD LOD_ODD, textureMode TRILINEAR + REPLACE);
- TMU0 samples the even LODs and blends the two by LOD fraction (TRILINEAR + TC/TCA_BLEND_LODFRAC);
- this uses both TMUs and disables 2ppc.
Point mip gets a +0.5 LOD bias. Two stages with LINEAR mip fail ValidateDevice with D3DERR_TOOMANYOPERATIONS unless LOD_DITHER is set (default 0); with it, TLODDITHER is used. Our caps advertise MIPFPOINT only.

Untextured triangles: specular is added to diffuse per vertex with CLAMP888. Textured triangles with non-zero specular are drawn again:
- blending ONE/ONE, or the app's source factor with destination ONE;
- ZFUNC EQUAL when Z writes are on;
- the FBI passes iterated RGB;
- fog switches to FOGADD.
Win9x later made untextured specular two-pass too, because a per-vertex clamp is wrong across an edge. 3dfx advertises SPECULARGOURAUDRGB and refuses D3DTA_SPECULAR as a stage argument. Ours ignores specular RGB.

DP2 CLEAR and Clear use FASTFILL:
- colour from color1;
- depth from zaColor (depth x mask);
- stencil through stencilMode CLEAR plus the reference.
Dither rotation is off during the fill. Afterwards TFACTOR is re-uploaded and the clip registers and zaColor are restored. Ours already uses fastfillCMD. It will need the zaColor restore once Z bias exists, and the stencil path once stencil exists.

3dfx streams everything through a command FIFO:
- state as PK1/PK4 packets with chip fields;
- textures as PK5;
- triangles as PK3 vertex runs, where sSetupMode chooses the parameters and strips/fans continue with CMD_CONT.
FIFO0 lives in the frame buffer, and AGP command FIFOs are compiled out. Ours writes every register over PCI behind VcrDdRoom, 9-15 writes per vertex.

3dfx:
- omits D3DPRASTERCAPS_ZTEST (commented out), although all Z functions work;
- never sets MIPMAPLODBIAS, although it implements it;
- never assigns dvMaxVertexW;
- advertises DESTALPHA/INVDESTALPHA/SRCALPHASAT, which it substitutes.
In the DX8 path, Caps2 is never assigned (a dangling 'D3DCAPS2_CANRENDERWINDOWED | 0;'), LineCaps is 0 and MaxPointSize is 1. Our caps should follow what we actually render, not 3dfx's list.

The revision logs and code name workarounds for:
- Unreal: palette, PRS 14371;
- UT: 3-stage remap, PRS 12099, Voodoo3 only;
- 3DMark 2000;
- Armor Command: ENALPHAMASK;
- Homeworld;
- F-22 Lightning 3: MAXMIPLEVEL;
- Centipede: fog;
- Hidden & Dangerous: clip init, PRS 12728;
- SpeedBusters: chromaRange init;
- Redline Racer, G-Police, Heavy Gear II;
- Forsaken: format order;
- MDK: lines;
- Quake III via the MS wrapper, and others.
Of the fleet's titles, Unreal Gold and UT 436 use Glide on .124 (D3D is a fallback there), and H&D Deluxe is a later D3D8 engine. None of these workarounds is known to be needed now.

The Win9x tree:
- fixes the TEXTURETRANSFORMFLAGS clobber (revision 100);
- keeps zEnable/zWriteEnable when no Z is attached and applies them when a Z arrives (revision 109, the DX8 water sample);
- draws lines -0.5..+0.5 (revision 9);
- makes untextured specular two-pass (revision 8, SPECULARFIX);
- fixes the LOD-dither chip mask (revision 108).
Where the trees differ, use the Win9x behaviour as the reference.

3dfx's validator refuses, with D3DERR_TOOMANYOPERATIONS or an UNSUPPORTED* code:
- more than 2 stages;
- multitexture with trilinear, unless LOD_DITHER is set;
- D3DTA_SPECULAR as an argument;
- unsupported ops and complements.
Ours returns OK with 1 pass for everything, so titles that probe with ValidateDevice pick setups that we then degrade silently. The UT 3-stage remap shows that 3dfx also met apps that never call it.

3dfx accepts TEXTURE|3DDEVICE surfaces: 565 everywhere, 8888/1555 on Napalm. Its heap placement:
- Z in TILED_HEAP1 when fullscreen;
- the flip chain in TILED_HEAP2;
- other 3D devices tiled first;
- textures linear first;
- tile-size alignment, or 16 bytes when linear.
Its DX8 list marks X8R8G8B8 as an offscreen render target. This pass did not compare our surface placement or render-to-texture handling.

understand:hw.summary:
I audited what the VSA-100 (Napalm) chip in the Voodoo 5 6000 on .124 can do for Direct3D, independent of any driver. The main source is 3dfx's own chip specification, which sits in the vendor tree as H5/DOCS/avenger_spec.doc ('Napalm High Performance Graphics Engine Rev 1.13'). I also used the H5 register headers, 3dfx's D3D feature matrix and its shipped W2K D3D driver, our h5 Glide fork and our vcr-kmd driver.
The work was read-only and no fleet box was contacted. The only file written is the report. I cite one on-box result taken from another agent's evidence.

Supported by the hardware but unused by our driver:
- single-stage trilinear (uses both TMUs and drops 2ppc);
- the extended combine: MODULATE2X/4X, ADDSIGNED(2X), SUBTRACT, ADDSMOOTH, BLEND*, and per-TMU TFACTOR;
- 8-bit stencil with all 8 ops (32 bpp only), and destination alpha at 32 bpp;
- constant Z-bias, W-buffer, mirror addressing, LOD bias, and a per-channel colour mask at 32 bpp;
- all 13 blend factors plus SUB and REVSUB;
- guard band, 1555 render targets, and more texture formats.

Largest performance gaps:
- 2 pixels per clock gives about 1.7x per chip for single-textured 16 bpp draws.
- Our D3D renders on chip 0 only, at 1 pixel per clock, while the board has 4-chip SLI.
- Commands go through PCI-FIFO register writes; a CMDFIFO with packet-3 strips and fans is available.

Impossible in the hardware:
- cube and volume maps, DOT3, EMBM, PREMODULATE;
- anisotropic filtering, border colour, MIN/MAX blend ops, slope-scaled bias;
- AGP texturing (the texture base address is local-only, and the board sits on PCI-66);
- non-power-of-2 textures and aspect ratios above 8:1;
- line and point primitives (draw them as triangles);
- mismatched colour and Z depths (allocating the Z at the target's depth works around it).

Our driver contradicts the spec in four places:
- Dither is left on at 32 bpp, which the spec forbids.
- SRCCOLOR and DESTCOLOR are mapped to ONE because 'the chip cannot'; blend factor 8 (SAMECOLOR) does exactly this.
- Vertex fog is approximated through a synthetic 1/W because 'the fog unit only knows W'; fogMode[4:3] feeds iterated W, Z or alpha directly.
- Line caps are advertised while every LINE and POINT opcode is skipped.

A 32 bpp target with D16 is refused by our ContextCreate, although the runtime reports the pair as matching. The on-box run measured CreateDevice failing with D3DERR_INVALIDCALL. 3dfx solved this by allocating the Z at 32 bits.

Open questions that need d3dprobe checks:
- what fogMode bits 3 and 4 do when both are set (the spec text and 3dfx's code disagree);
- whether P8 and A8P8 work at all (the vintage driver lane saw P8 sample white and compiled it out);
- whether a stale stencil enable is ignored at 16 bpp;
- whether packed sARGB reaches a TMU;
- how much 2ppc gains at 32 bpp and high resolutions;
- projected textures;
- D3D on SLI.

items.detail:
With cmdFifo0 off:
- fullscreen 1024x768x32 + D24X8: 40/40;
- fullscreen 640x480x16 + D16: 40/40;
- windowed on the 32 bpp desktop: 42/42;
- textures A at 1024x768x32: 83/0, 0 skipped. Covers mip (levels 0-2), mip2048 (levels 0-4, 2048..128), tex512/1024/2048, A8R8G8B8 64/256/512/512x128, DXT1/3/5 128, dxt1_512, dxt5 512/512x256/512x128/256x128; LockRect pitch equals the block row;
- textures B: 37/3. Every decode (dxt1/3/5, decode1, rgb565_decode, rgb565_decode1 at 1:1, dxt5_big a/b/c windows, texhigh above 16 MB) has 0 of 1024 texels wrong in both RGB and alpha, so the half-pixel fix holds on silicon.

The 3 failures are dxt5_512x128_def, dxt5_128_def and tex8888_512x128_def: LockRect 8876086c on a D3DPOOL_DEFAULT texture, which the D3D8 runtime refuses. That is a probe expectation, not a HAL defect.
